    "cost_closing_html":  "Lean beef and a can of tomato sauce do most of the work, which keeps this one easy on the cost tab. The olives and capers are the pantry splurge, and a small can or jar of each covers the batch.",
    "shop_smart":  [
                       "\u003cstrong\u003eGreen olives and capers are the briny backbone, so buy the jars.\u003c/strong\u003e A can of olives and a small jar of capers cost a fraction of the olive bar and last through several batches in the fridge.",
                       "\u003cstrong\u003eTomato sauce is the cheap base.\u003c/strong\u003e A small can builds the whole simmer for almost nothing, and it keeps in the pantry until you need it.",
                       "\u003cstrong\u003eOne bell pepper and one onion is all it takes.\u003c/strong\u003e Add a handful of raisins from the baking aisle for the sweet note. This is a light-produce recipe, so you are not buying a cart of vegetables to make it work."
                   ],
    "make_it":  [
                    "Weigh your empty mixing pot and write the number down for portioning later.",
                    "Cook the rice per the package and set it aside.",
                    "Brown the ground beef in a large skillet, breaking it up as it cooks, then add the diced onion and red bell pepper and cook until they soften.",
                    "Stir in the tomato sauce, the chopped fresh tomato, the raisins, the cumin, oregano, and salt, and simmer 10 minutes until it thickens and the raisins plump.",
                    "Slice the green olives and stir them in with the capers for the last couple of minutes so they keep their bite.",
                    "Fold the cooked rice into the filling and warm everything through.",
                    "Warm the tortillas, then portion and wrap as below."
                ],
    "portion_html":  "Set the full pot on your scale and subtract the empty pot weight from Step 1. That is your total filling weight. Divide it by 14, spoon that amount onto each warm tortilla, and roll. Every burrito lands at about \u003cstrong\u003e{{cal}} calories and {{protein}} grams of protein\u003c/strong\u003e.",
